subsections.content:
PermaClaw supports multiple chat platforms. Configure them in `~/.permaclaw/config.json`.

{
  "channels": {
    "telegram": {
      "enabled": true,
      "token": "YOUR_BOT_TOKEN"
    },
    "discord": {
      "enabled": true,
      "token": "YOUR_BOT_TOKEN"
    }
  }
}

After configuration, run `permaclaw` (or `permaclaw gateway`) and the channels will be active.
For detailed setup of each channel, see the Chat Apps section below.

The main configuration file is `~/.permaclaw/config.json`. You can override its location with the `PERMACLAW_CONFIG` environment variable.

A minimal example:
{
  "wallet_path": "/home/user/.aos.json",
  "aostore": {
    "process_id": "aoStore-xxxxxx",
    "ao_node_url": "https://ao.arweave.net"
  },
  "aomem": {
    "process_id": ""
  },
  "agents": {
    "defaults": {
      "model": "gpt-5.2"
    }
  },
  "model_list": [
    {
      "model_name": "gpt-5.2",
      "model": "openai/gpt-5.2",
      "api_key": "sk-..."
    }
  ]
}

See the full documentation for all available options.

Configure multiple endpoints for the same model to distribute load:

{
  "model_list": [
    {
      "model_name": "gpt4",
      "model": "openai/gpt-5.4",
      "api_key": "sk-key1",
      "api_base": "https://api1.example.com/v1"
    },
    {
      "model_name": "gpt4",
      "model": "openai/gpt-5.4",
      "api_key": "sk-key2",
      "api_base": "https://api2.example.com/v1"
    },
    {
      "model_name": "gpt4",
      "model": "openai/gpt-5.4",
      "api_key": "sk-key3",
      "api_base": "https://api3.example.com/v1"
    }
  ]
}

When you request model `gpt4`, requests will be distributed across all three endpoints using round-robin selection.

This provides:
- Automatic failover if one endpoint fails
- Better rate limit distribution
- Improved reliability for production deployments

Add this to `config.json`:

{
  "channels": {
    "matrix": {
      "enabled": true,
      "homeserver": "https://matrix.org",
      "user_id": "@your-bot:matrix.org",
      "access_token": "YOUR_MATRIX_ACCESS_TOKEN",
      "device_id": "",
      "join_on_invite": true,
      "allow_from": [],
      "group_trigger": {
        "mention_only": true
      },
      "placeholder": {
        "enabled": true,
        "text": "Thinking..."
      },
      "reasoning_channel_id": "",
      "message_format": "richtext"
    }
  }
}

The Provider Architecture has been refactored to support a model-centric configuration approach.

Current Problems Solved
Configuration Structure Issues: Old approach required modifying Go code for each new provider
Code Bloat Trend: Each OpenAI-compatible provider required changes to config.go and http_provider.go
Agent-Provider Coupling: Agents needed to know both provider and model, adding complexity

New Approach: model_list
Inspired by LiteLLM design:
Model-centric: Users care about models, not providers
Protocol prefix: Use `protocol/model_name` format, e.g., `openai/gpt-5.4`, `anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6`
Configuration-driven: Adding new providers only requires config changes, no code changes

Multi-Agent Compatibility
{
  "model_list": [...],
  "agents": {
    "defaults": {
      "model": "deepseek-chat"
    },
    "coder": {
      "model": "gpt-5.4",
      "system_prompt": "You are a coding assistant..."
    },
    "translator": {
      "model": "claude-sonnet-4.6"
    }
  }
}
Each agent only needs to specify `model` (corresponds to `model_name` in `model_list`).

With `model_list`, adding a new OpenAI-compatible provider requires zero code changes:

{
  "model_list": [
    {
      "model_name": "my-custom-llm",
      "model": "openai/my-model-v1",
      "api_key": "your-api-key",
      "api_base": "https://api.your-provider.com/v1"
    }
  ]
}

Just specify `openai/` as the protocol (or omit it for the default), and provide your provider's API base URL.

For providers requiring special authentication (like Antigravity OAuth), use the `auth_method` field:

{
  "model_list": [
    {
      "model_name": "gemini-flash",
      "model": "antigravity/gemini-3-flash",
      "auth_method": "oauth"
    }
  ]
}

Then authenticate with:
permaclaw auth login --provider antigravity

config.json Configuration
{
  "model_list": [
    {
      "model_name": "gemini-flash",
      "model": "antigravity/gemini-3-flash",
      "auth_method": "oauth"
    }
  ],
  "agents": {
    "defaults": {
      "model": "gemini-flash"
    }
  }
}

Auth Profile Storage
Auth profiles are stored in `~/.permaclaw/auth.json`:
{
  "credentials": {
    "google-antigravity": {
      "access_token": "ya29...",
      "refresh_token": "1//...",
      "expires_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
      "provider": "google-antigravity",
      "auth_method": "oauth",
      "email": "user@example.com",
      "project_id": "my-project-id"
    }
  }
}

Environment Variables
export PICOCLAW_AGENTS_DEFAULTS_MODEL=gemini-flash

Create a bot with @BotFather and copy the token.
Get your user ID from @userinfobot.
Add to config:
"channels": {
  "telegram": {
    "enabled": true,
    "token": "YOUR_TOKEN",
    "allow_from": ["YOUR_USER_ID"]
  }
}
Run `permaclaw gateway`. The bot will automatically register commands (`/start`, `/help`, etc.).
